## Summary

Wire role-based routing configuration (skills, path patterns, max priority) from RoleConfig through the daemon's buildCommand as environment variables, so built-in role subprocesses (loom plan, loom task) can reconstruct RoleConstraints and use the scored task router (BuildRouterTaskCheck/SelectBestTask). Currently, buildCommand passes LOOM_ALLOWED_TOOLS/LOOM_DENIED_TOOLS/LOOM_READ_ONLY but NOT the routing-critical fields. Additionally, resolveRoleConfig ignores user-configured RoleConfig values for built-in roles, silently dropping skills/paths/priority configs from loom.yaml.

## Technical Approach

**Two-sided change**: daemon-side (sending env vars) and subprocess-side (reading env vars + using the task router).

**Key design decisions**:
1. Fix resolveRoleConfig to merge user config ON TOP of built-in defaults (not ignore it)
2. Add env vars in buildCommand for skills, path_patterns, max_priority, task_filter, and role name
3. Add RoleConfigFromEnv()/AgentEntryFromEnv() functions that reconstruct config from env vars
4. Decouple CustomTaskCheck from CustomPromptGen in automode.go so routing works without wrapping built-in prompts
5. Wire the reconstructed config into plan.go/task.go via BuildRouterTaskCheck
6. Backward compatible: when no routing env vars are set, behavior is identical to current

**LOC constraint**: daemon.go is at its 1091 LOC allowlist limit. All new functions go into task_router.go (184 LOC, natural home for routing logic) or a new daemon_role_merge.go file. Only minimal changes (env var append lines) touch daemon.go, offset by extracting resolveRoleConfig out.

