## Summary

Add tab overflow handling with scroll arrow buttons and keyboard shortcuts to the terminal tab bar. When tabs overflow the visible area, left/right scroll arrow buttons appear. Ctrl+Tab and Ctrl+Shift+Tab cycle tabs (with Alt+Arrow fallback for Firefox). Escape returns to the previous view when no modal/search is open. The existing MAX_TABS = 8 limit (defined in terminalTabUtils.ts:8, enforced at TerminalView.tsx:323) is unchanged.

## Revision Notes

This design addresses all feedback from both reviews:
1. **tabListRef ownership resolved** — overflow detection and scroll buttons are entirely within TerminalTabBar, which already owns the tabListRef (line 41). No ref prop is added; no forwardRef needed.
2. **No Cmd+W** — this task does NOT add Cmd+W. That shortcut belongs to uffak.19 (context menu) and uffak.22 (accessibility). No conflict.
3. **Firefox Ctrl+Tab limitation acknowledged** — Firefox intercepts Ctrl+Tab at the browser/OS level before JavaScript. e.preventDefault() cannot override this. We add Alt+ArrowRight / Alt+ArrowLeft as a cross-browser fallback. The design documents this limitation explicitly.
4. **Coordination with uffak.22** — both tasks extend the same keydown useEffect in TerminalView.tsx (lines 249-275). This task adds Ctrl+Tab/Ctrl+Shift+Tab, Alt+Arrow, and Escape-to-kanban. uffak.22 adds Cmd+1-9, Cmd+T, Cmd+W, focus zones, and ARIA live regions. The handlers are independent if-blocks and do not conflict regardless of implementation order.
5. **pendingPasteText verified** — it exists at TerminalView.tsx:73, destructured from useClipboard(). Already referenced in the existing Escape handler at line 260. Not a phantom reference.
6. **handleBackendSelect verified** — it exists at TerminalView.tsx:327. The reviewer may have been looking at an older version.
7. **previousView verified** — it exists at App.tsx:269 as useState<ViewMode>("kanban"). The onEscape callback will compile.

## Technical Approach

**Overflow detection and scroll buttons (TerminalTabBar-internal):** All overflow logic stays inside TerminalTabBar since it already owns tabListRef (line 41). A new internal useEffect uses ResizeObserver + scroll event listener on the .tabList div to track { canScrollLeft, canScrollRight } state. Scroll arrow buttons render conditionally flanking the tab list. This is a pure TerminalTabBar concern — no props added, no ref exposed.

**Ctrl+Tab / Ctrl+Shift+Tab and Alt+Arrow (TerminalView):** Added to the existing keydown useEffect (lines 249-275). Ctrl+Tab moves to the next tab (wrapping), Ctrl+Shift+Tab to the previous. Alt+ArrowRight / Alt+ArrowLeft provide a Firefox-compatible fallback that works across all browsers. e.preventDefault() is called on all to suppress default browser behavior where possible.

**Escape returns to previous view (TerminalView + App.tsx):** A new onEscape prop on TerminalViewProps. In the existing keydown handler, when Escape is pressed and !isSearchOpen && !isSessionPromptOpen && pendingPasteText === null, call onEscape(). App.tsx passes () => setActiveView(previousView || "kanban"), using the existing previousView state at line 269.

**Auto-scroll new tab into view (TerminalTabBar):** A useEffect watches tabs.length. When it increases, scrolls tabListRef to the far right so the newly added tab is visible. This replaces the old design's approach of doing this in handleBackendSelect (which would have required exposing the ref).

