- **No external packages needed** — uses native ResizeObserver, scrollBy(), scrollTo() APIs (supported in all modern browsers)
- **Internal modules** — TerminalTabBar, TerminalView, App.tsx modified in place
- **No prerequisite tasks** — all infrastructure exists

## Edge Cases & Error Handling

1. **≤5 tabs (no overflow):** Scroll buttons never appear. Ctrl+Tab/Alt+Arrow still work for cycling. UI is identical to current behavior.
2. **Exactly at scroll boundary:** 1px tolerance in overflow detection (scrollLeft > 1, scrollLeft + clientWidth < scrollWidth - 1) prevents flickering from sub-pixel rounding.
3. **Single tab:** Ctrl+Tab/Alt+Arrow does nothing (no-op when tabs.length <= 1). Escape still returns to previous view.
4. **Tab added at MAX_TABS (8):** New tab button already returns early at MAX_TABS (line 323). Scroll buttons appear for 6+ tabs. New tabs auto-scroll into view.
5. **Tab closed while overflowing:** ResizeObserver fires, overflow state recalculated. If tabs now fit, scroll buttons disappear.
6. **Rapid Ctrl+Tab presses:** Each press synchronously reads tabs via setTabs callback. No race conditions or stale closures.
7. **Escape priority chain:** Escape is only handled for view-switching when !isSearchOpen && !isSessionPromptOpen && pendingPasteText === null. The existing Escape handler for search (lines 256-265) fires first in the same handler function. Search overlay, backend picker prompt, and paste confirmation dialog all take priority.
8. **Firefox Ctrl+Tab:** Cannot be intercepted — Firefox handles it at the browser level before JavaScript. Alt+ArrowRight / Alt+ArrowLeft fallback works on all browsers including Firefox. The Ctrl+Tab handler is still registered so it works on Chrome, Edge, and Safari.
9. **Tab rename in progress:** When editingTabId !== null in TerminalTabBar, keyboard events in the rename input use e.stopPropagation() (line 111), so Ctrl+Tab and Escape won't interfere with rename editing.
10. **Smooth scroll overlap:** Multiple rapid clicks on scroll buttons queue smooth scrolls. Browsers handle this gracefully (new scrollBy supersedes in-progress scroll). Overflow state updates on each scroll event.
11. **onEscape not provided:** The optional chaining (onEscape?.()) means the Escape handler is a no-op if the prop isn't passed. TerminalView can be used without the escape feature.
12. **previousView is "terminal":** This edge case can't happen — setPreviousView is only called before navigating to issue-detail (line 445), and the terminal view doesn't navigate to issue-detail internally.

