## Edge Cases & Error Handling

### 1. fetchSseToken() network failure
If the server is unreachable, get() throws a network error (ApiError with status 0). fetchSseToken() catches this and returns { kind: "error", message: "..." }. connect() emits the error via onError callback and sets state to disconnected. The consumer sees the connection error and can retry later.

### 2. fetchSseToken() returns 401 (JWT expired)
The get() helper already has a 401 interceptor (client.ts line 260-267) that clears the token, calls initAuth(), and retries once. If the retry succeeds (new JWT obtained), fetchSseToken() gets the opaque token successfully. If retry fails, get() throws ApiError(401), and fetchSseToken() returns { kind: "error" }. connect() shows the error.

### 3. Opaque token expires before EventSource connects
The opaque token has a 30-second TTL (per .20 design). If the browser takes >30s to establish the EventSource connection (extremely unlikely for same-origin), the server rejects with 401. The EventSource fires an error, handleError() closes it (usesOpaqueToken is true), and sets state to reconnecting. The consumer calls retryNow() → connect() → fetches fresh token.

### 4. Browser auto-reconnect with stale opaque token
Prevented by the handleError() change: when usesOpaqueToken is true, the EventSource is explicitly closed on error, preventing the browser from auto-reconnecting with the same (now-used) URL. The consumer's retryNow() calls connect() which fetches a fresh token.

### 5. retryNow() in open mode (no opaque token)
retryNow() calls connect() which calls fetchSseToken(). In open mode, fetchSseToken() returns { kind: "disabled" }, and connect() creates EventSource without a token. This is identical to the current behavior.

### 6. retryNow() in external mode
retryNow() closes the old EventSource and calls connect(). connect() calls fetchSseToken() which fetches a fresh opaque token, then creates a new EventSource with the fresh token. This works correctly because each connect() fetches a new single-use token.

### 7. Sign-out while SSE is connecting
If signOut() fires auth-sign-out while connect() is awaiting fetchSseToken(), the disconnect() call sets manualDisconnect = true and closes any existing EventSource. When fetchSseToken() resolves and connect() tries to create the EventSource, the method should check for cancellation. Add check: after fetchSseToken() returns, if this.manualDisconnect is true, bail out early.

### 8. Sign-out while SSE is reconnecting
If the SSE client is in "reconnecting" state and auth-sign-out fires, disconnect() closes the EventSource and sets state to disconnected. The consumer's retryNow() would be a no-op (state check fails). Clean and correct.

### 9. Multiple sign-out events
disconnect() is idempotent. Multiple auth-sign-out events are harmless — the second disconnect() call is a no-op when already disconnected.

### 10. Sign-out in open mode (NoAuthProvider)
NoAuthProvider's signOut() is a no-op (per .16 design), so auth-sign-out is never dispatched. The listeners exist but are never triggered. No overhead beyond the addEventListener/removeEventListener calls.

### 11. WebSocket close during sign-out
The TerminalInstance sign-out handler calls wsCleanupRef.current?.(). This calls the inner cleanup function in terminalConnection.ts which closes the WebSocket with code 1000 (normal closure). The backend sees a clean close. No reconnection is attempted because AuthGate unmounts the terminal component.

### 12. Race: connect() in progress when disconnect() called
connect() is async. If disconnect() is called while connect() is awaiting fetchSseToken(), we need to bail out after the await. Add a check: after the fetchSseToken() await, check if this.manualDisconnect is true or this.state is "disconnected", and if so, return early without creating the EventSource.

