## Problem

renderPrompt (internal/cli/prompts.go:38-54) contains three panics. Two are safe (embedded resources validated at compile/test time), but the third (line 50-51, tmpl.Execute) is reachable at runtime when a user-provided override template in loom-prompts/*.md references struct fields that don't exist on promptTemplateData.

## Previous Design Issues

The previous design inlined the embedded template loading logic directly in the Execute fallback path, duplicating what loadTemplate already does. It also didn't track whether an override was used, meaning if the embedded template itself failed Execute, it would pointlessly retry with the same content.

## Approach: Track Override Origin, Fallback Only When Warranted

Modify loadTemplate to return whether the content came from an override. On Execute failure, only attempt embedded fallback if an override was active. If the embedded template fails Execute, that's a real bug — keep the panic.

## Implementation

### File: internal/cli/prompts.go

**Change 1: loadTemplate signature (lines 63-83)**

Change return type from (string, error) to (string, bool, error) where the bool indicates whether an override was loaded:

    func loadTemplate(name string) (content string, isOverride bool, err error) {
        base := promptOverrideDir
        if base == "" {
            base = "."
        }
        overridePath := filepath.Join(base, "loom-prompts", name+".md")
        if data, err := os.ReadFile(overridePath); err == nil {
            if _, parseErr := template.New(name).Parse(string(data)); parseErr != nil {
                log.Printf("warning: invalid template override %s: %v, using embedded default", overridePath, parseErr)
            } else {
                return string(data), true, nil
            }
        }
        data, err := promptFS.ReadFile("prompts/" + name + ".md")
        if err != nil {
            return "", false, fmt.Errorf("embedded template %q not found: %w", name, err)
        }
        return string(data), false, nil
    }

**Change 2: renderPrompt Execute fallback (lines 38-54)**

Update the caller to use the new return value and add fallback logic:

    func renderPrompt(name string, data promptTemplateData) string {
        tmplContent, isOverride, err := loadTemplate(name)
        if err != nil {
            panic(fmt.Sprintf("prompt: failed to load template %q: %v", name, err))
        }

        tmpl, err := template.New(name).Parse(tmplContent)
        if err != nil {
            panic(fmt.Sprintf("prompt: failed to parse template %q: %v", name, err))
        }

        var buf bytes.Buffer
        if err := tmpl.Execute(&buf, data); err != nil {
            if !isOverride {
                panic(fmt.Sprintf("prompt: failed to execute template %q: %v", name, err))
            }
            log.Printf("warning: override template %q execution failed: %v; falling back to embedded default", name, err)
            embedded, _, loadErr := loadTemplate(name)
            // Force embedded path: temporarily clear override dir isn't needed since
            // we can read embedded directly
            embData, embErr := promptFS.ReadFile("prompts/" + name + ".md")
            if embErr != nil {
                panic(fmt.Sprintf("prompt: embedded template %q not found: %v", name, embErr))
            }
            _ = embedded   // unused, we read directly
            _ = loadErr    // unused
            embTmpl, parseErr := template.New(name).Parse(string(embData))
            if parseErr != nil {
                panic(fmt.Sprintf("prompt: embedded template %q parse failed: %v", name, parseErr))
            }
            buf.Reset()
            if execErr := embTmpl.Execute(&buf, data); execErr != nil {
                panic(fmt.Sprintf("prompt: embedded template %q execute failed: %v", name, execErr))
            }
        }
        return buf.String()
    }

Wait — I'm still duplicating embedded loading. Let me simplify by reading embedded directly (promptFS.ReadFile) in the fallback, which is the minimal addition. loadTemplate's extra bool return cleanly separates the concern.

**Revised Change 2 (cleaner):**

    func renderPrompt(name string, data promptTemplateData) string {
        tmplContent, isOverride, err := loadTemplate(name)
        if err != nil {
            panic(fmt.Sprintf("prompt: failed to load template %q: %v", name, err))
        }

        tmpl, err := template.New(name).Parse(tmplContent)
        if err != nil {
            panic(fmt.Sprintf("prompt: failed to parse template %q: %v", name, err))
        }

        var buf bytes.Buffer
        if err := tmpl.Execute(&buf, data); err != nil {
            if !isOverride {
                panic(fmt.Sprintf("prompt: failed to execute template %q: %v", name, err))
            }
            log.Printf("warning: override template %q execution failed: %v; falling back to embedded default", name, err)
            embContent, embErr := promptFS.ReadFile("prompts/" + name + ".md")
            if embErr != nil {
                panic(fmt.Sprintf("prompt: embedded fallback template %q not found: %v", name, embErr))
            }
            embTmpl := template.Must(template.New(name).Parse(string(embContent)))
            buf.Reset()
            if execErr := embTmpl.Execute(&buf, data); execErr != nil {
                panic(fmt.Sprintf("prompt: embedded template %q execute failed (bug): %v", name, execErr))
            }
        }
        return buf.String()
    }

